# Renode script: simulate an STM32F4 Discovery board and load our firmware.
# Run from the repo root:  renode renode/stm32f4.resc
using sysbus

mach create "stm32f4"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/stm32f4_discovery-kit.repl

# Day 4: this board file's nvic (which also models the Cortex-M SysTick
# timer) defaults to a 72 MHz systick clock -- that assumes a firmware
# that has already configured the PLL for a fast core clock. Ours never
# touches the PLL, so it's still running on the 16 MHz HSI reset clock
# that drivers/systick.c's reload-value calculation assumes. Without
# this override, SysTick would fire ~4.5x faster than the firmware
# expects and the LED would blink at ~111 ms instead of 500 ms.
sysbus.nvic Frequency 16000000

# Day 4: show the green user LED's (PD12) state changes in the console
# log, so the 500 ms-spaced toggles are visible without extra hardware.
# -1 selects the NOISY log level, which is what LED state changes log at.
logLevel -1 sysbus.gpioPortD.UserLED

$bin ?= @build/firmware.elf
sysbus LoadELF $bin

# Route USART2 output to the console (used by `make run`, which passes
# --console) instead of opening a separate terminal window. Each line is
# timestamped with both host and *virtual* (simulated MCU) time, e.g.
# "virt: 2.5s" -- the LED toggle message printed over UART below uses
# that virtual timestamp to confirm the 500 ms period in firmware time,
# independent of however fast the host happens to run the simulation.
showAnalyzer sysbus.usart2
